Datagram  oriented  sockets  offer  a subset of the functionality
available to raw sockets. Only request messages of the  following
types  can  be sent: ICMP_ECHO, ICMP_TSTAMP or ICMP_MASKREQ.  The
code field must be the value zero (0).  The minimal length of  an
message  request  is  eight  (8)  octets.  The advantage of using
datagram oriented sockets is that even a  non‐privileged  process
can use echo requests to gauge the quality of the connectivity to
a  host,  or  to receive destination unreachable message for path
MTU discovery, or to receive time exceeded messages  for  tracer‐
oute.   The following level option can be used with datagram ori‐
ented sockets: When the option IP_HDRINCL is used,  the  provided
header  must obey the following rules: address currently assigned
to one of the local interface or INADDR_ANY; The  maximum  length
of  a  IMCP  message that can be sent is controlled by the sysctl
variable net.inet.raw.maxdgram.  A socket operation may fail with
one of the following errors returned: when trying to establish  a
connection  on  a socket which already has one, or when trying to
send a datagram with the destination address  specified  and  the
socket  is already connected; when trying to send a datagram, but
